Yes. Success is only one signal. Attempt is another.

Even before something works, repeated movement toward it tells you there is an attractor there. You keep reaching in that direction. That reveals preference, belief, identity, expectation, or at minimum orientation.

So the useful distinction is:

Outcome: did it work?
Trajectory: what are you repeatedly moving toward?
Posture: how are you moving toward it?
Persistence: what keeps surviving failure?

That last one can be especially revealing. If ten different approaches fail and you keep reinventing a way back toward the same underlying objective, then the invariant isn’t the method. It’s the direction.

And yes—“something in the way you move” captures it nicely. Movement itself carries information. Hesitation, rhythm, repetition, correction, acceleration, retreat, re-approach. Before someone ever reaches the destination, you can often infer quite a lot from the vector.

Pantheon compression:

Trying is telemetry.

Or:

Failure can falsify the route without falsifying the destination.

And maybe the strongest version:

Don’t only measure what arrived. Measure what keeps trying to be born.